>[]($block.attrs("author-note"))
> I spent the first 2 weeks of June doing a whirlwind tour of 4 different cities in the
> US, followed by returning to Amsterdam just in time for the generational heat wave that
> has swept through Europe, so this month was pretty light on most things.

# Stuff I Did
## Ran
I didn't run at all while I was in the states (though I did do at least a bit of hiking
and a lot of walking around), so my stats are pretty shit for the month. I've also been
having a bit of knee pain that I think has to do with my stride, so all in all it actually
might have been a good thing to reduce mileage for a while.

```
❯ thoth summary --since month
=== THIS MONTH ===
distance (km): 33.521
average speed (km/hr): 10.711
total_time (hr): 3.20⏎
```

## Played
Even though I spent an ungodly amount of time on a plane or in an airport this month, I
only got through one (very short) full game:
* [*En Garde!*](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1654660/En_Garde/), which was very fun
and just long enough not to overstay its welcome. Which is good, because by the end I was
getting a little bit fed up with the "giant horde of enemies" combat loop, which would have
tainted an otherwise charming and cheesy version of the swordfight scene in Princess
Bride.
* I did play a lot of [Vampire Survivors](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1794680/Vampire_Survivors/)
and [Absolum](https://store.steampowered.com/app/1904480/Absolum/) during my travels as
well, but since neither can really be "completed" (I guess technically Absolum can, but
I've already finished the main story there), they mostly just served as time killers.

## Read
### Books
I did get to do a fair bit of reading in June, especially because I spent so much time on
planes and in airports. In order, I finished:
* *[Ancillary Justice](https://hardcover.app/books/ancillary-justice)*, which had been on
my list for a while as the only winner of both the Hugo and Nebula awards in the same
year. I have to say, though, it was... dissapointing. It has all the right ingredients -
cool premise, interesting characters, really neat twist with the linguistics - but it just
kinda ponders onwards and onwards and nothing really happens for a while. It's definitely
not *bad*, it's just not anything super outstanding, at least in my opinion. I'll probably
finish the trilogy eventually, but other stuff is gonna go on the stack first.
* *[For We Are Many](https://hardcover.app/books/for-we-are-many)* was my other plane
book. Kinda similar thing, honestly. The Bobiverse is fun and it has lots of interesting
concepts, but already on the second book it feels like something I can only describe as
"competence porn." It really, really reads like it was written by a software engineer
(which it was) who thinks of themselves as smarter than the average bear (maybe he does, I
can't make that claim becuase I don't know him). Again, I'll probably finish the series
eventually, but for now the Bobiverse is on the backburner (backbober? bobburner? there's
a pun here somewhere).
* Needing a bit of a break from sci-fi when I got home, I read *[Factfulness](https://hardcover.app/books/factfulness-585fef27-4331-49bf-83c1-75d35129c879)*
after seeing it recommended by Jamie Brandon on his [Reads that Lasted](https://www.scattered-thoughts.net/reads).
I thought it was really good! It came at a good time for me too (in the midst of the world
currently losing its mind over anything and everything AI, the president of my country
doing *gestures vaugely at everything*, etc). I've been trying to reframe a lot of how
I've been thinking in the last couple weeks to look more towards "the slow march of
progress" rather than "everything is shit and we should just accept it and die."
* Finally, I slammed through *[What I Talk About When I Talk About Running](https://hardcover.app/books/what-i-talk-about-when-i-talk-about-running)*,
which has (yet again) been on my read list for forever. This one seems to be super
divisive: lots of people love it to death, lots of people think it's meandering and boring
and so on. Which means that I can very clearly spot who is a distance runner and who isn't
(not a criticism). I loved it. Fantastic read, he basically nails every single reason I
love the sport, and somehow managed to put into very nice prose within the first couple
chapters the thing that I always struggle to explain when people ask me about running: My
head is basically empty during a long run. I often get home and can't remember anything I thought
about. For me, this is a feature, not a bug. Murakami gets me. He and I could have a beer.

### Not Books
I didn't really read anything non-book of note this month (or at least nothing I cared to
remember or write down or bookmark), which was probably a good thing and a needed break.
But I did think about this quote a lot (one of my favorites) related to some stuff going
on at work:

> "The odds are greatly against you being immensely smarter than everyone else in the
> field. If your analysis says your terminal velocity is twice the speed of light, you may
> have invented warp drive, but the chances are a lot better that you've screwed up."
>
> -- [Akin’s Laws of Spacecraft Design #19](https://www.ece.uvic.ca/~elec399/201409/Akin%27s%20Laws%20of%20Spacecraft%20Design.pdf)

